Let $T,B\geq1$ and $m\geq2$ be integers, and let $\Gamma_1,\ldots,\Gamma_m$ be pairwise disjoint alphabets. For $1\leq a,b\leq T$ and $1\leq i\leq m$, take $X_{a,i},Y_{b,i}\in\Gamma_i^B$, and define $$X=\prod_{a=1}^T(X_{a,1}\cdots X_{a,m}),\qquad
 Y=\prod_{b=1}^T(Y_{b,1}\cdots Y_{b,m}),\qquad N=TmB.$$ Suppose $d\geq0$ is real and $$\label{eq:local-tagged}
 \sum_{i=1}^m r_B(X_{a,i},Y_{b,i})\geq d
 \qquad(1\leq a,b\leq T).$$ With $g=(m-1)B$, one has $$\label{eq:tagged-result}
 r_N(X,Y)\geq\frac{Tdg}{d+g}
 \geq\frac T2\min(d,g).$$ In particular, $d\leq g$ implies $r_N(X,Y)\geq Td/2$.

If $F:A\to\ell_1$ is any map on a finite abelian group, then there are weights $H(\chi)\geq0$ such that, for every $z\in A$, $$\label{eq:finite-fourier}
 \mathbb E_x\|F(x+z)-F(x)\|_1
 =\sum_{\chi\in\widehat A}|1-\chi(z)|^2H(\chi).$$ The map $F$ need not be injective.

For all sufficiently large $k$, every node $v$ and every fixed $t\in\mathbb T$ with $|t|_{\mathbb T}\geq\epsilon$ satisfy $$\mathbb P[t\text{ is good at }v]\geq9/10,$$ where probability is over the construction in the subtree below $v$. Consequently, there exists a realization of the entire tree for which $$\label{eq:torus-separation}
 r_N(X(w),X(w+1/2))\geq\gamma^kN
 \qquad\text{for every }w\in\mathbb T^{m^k}.$$

Let $n\geq1$ be an integer and let $F:\mathbb T^n\to\ell_1$ be a measurable map with finite range. Set $$L_F(h)=\mathbb E_w\|F(w+h)-F(w)\|_1
 \qquad(h\in\mathbb T^n),$$ using normalized Haar measure. There are summable coefficients $\mu_\xi\geq0$, indexed by $\xi\in\mathbb Z^n$, such that $$\label{eq:torus-fourier}
 L_F(h)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathbb Z^n}
 \mu_\xi\bigl(1-\cos(2\pi\langle\xi,h\rangle)\bigr)
 \qquad(h\in\mathbb T^n).$$

For the fixed construction and its shifts $h_v(a)$, every measurable finite-range $F:\mathbb T^{m^k}\to\ell_1$ satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
 L_F(e_o/2)\leq{}&
 4\sum_{v\text{ all nodes}}
 \mathbb E_{\eta\in[0,1/Q]}L_F(\eta e_v)
 +2\sum_{v\text{ internal}}
 \mathbb E_{a\in\mathbb Z/P_v\mathbb Z}L_F(h_v(a))\notag\\
 &\quad+2k^{-k}\sum_{u\text{ leaf}}
 \mathbb E_{\eta\in\mathbb T}L_F(\eta e_u).
 \label{eq:detection}
\end{aligned}$$ Every interval, finite-group and circle average has total mass one.

There is an absolute integer $M_0$ such that, for every integer $M\geq M_0$ and every alphabet $\Gamma$ with $1\leq K=|\Gamma|\leq M$, there exists a map $c:\Gamma\to\{0,1\}^{P}$ with $P=M^2$ whose concatenation extension $\operatorname{enc}$ satisfies $$\label{eq:804}
 \frac P{804}\operatorname{ED}(x,y)
 \leq\operatorname{ED}(\operatorname{enc}(x),\operatorname{enc}(y))\leq P\operatorname{ED}(x,y)
 \qquad(x,y\in\Gamma^M).$$ The encoded words have length $MP=M^3$. More precisely, $$\label{eq:402}
 r_{MP}(\operatorname{enc}(x),\operatorname{enc}(y))\geq\frac P{402}r_M(x,y)
 \qquad(x,y\in\Gamma^M).$$

For the realization fixed after Proposition <a href="#prop:good-probability" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:good-probability">13</a>, the following estimates hold, with $q$ denoting the depth of $v$ in the first two statements.*


1.  *If $v$ is internal and $a$ is an integer with $0\leq a<P_v$, then for every $w$, $$\label{eq:cycle-cost}
     \operatorname{ED}(X(w),X(w+h_v(a)))\leq2Nm^{-q}\frac{P_v}{T}.$$*


2.  *If $v$ is any node and $0\leq\eta\leq1/Q$, then $$\label{eq:tiny-cost}
     \mathbb E_w\operatorname{ED}(X(w),X(w+\eta e_v))\leq Nm^{-q}\frac RQ,$$ where $w$ has normalized Haar measure on $\mathbb T^{m^k}$.*


3.  *If $u$ is a leaf, then for every $w$ and $\eta\in\mathbb T$, $$\label{eq:leaf-cost}
     \operatorname{ED}(X(w),X(w+\eta e_u))\leq Nm^{-k}.$$

